1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,710
A miter saw kicked, and the
blade caught his forearm.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:09,090
The blood is bright red, and it's
pumping out with every heartbeat, right

3
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:11,777
through the gauze under her hand.

4
00:00:11,827 --> 00:00:15,203
A bleed like this can
kill in a few minutes.

5
00:00:15,253 --> 00:00:18,200
So what do your hands do, right now?

6
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:23,597
Stick with me, and you'll know which
bleeds you just press on, which ones

7
00:00:23,647 --> 00:00:28,237
get a tourniquet right away, where it
goes and how tight, and when you pack

8
00:00:28,287 --> 00:00:29,327
a wound instead.

9
00:00:29,827 --> 00:00:34,088
If this feels confusing, it's usually
because of what people learned years

10
00:00:34,138 --> 00:00:38,949
ago. Tourniquets used to be the last
resort, because people thought they

11
00:00:38,999 --> 00:00:42,549
cost the limb. Military
experience flipped that.

12
00:00:42,599 --> 00:00:47,674
If you put one on right, and it's on
for the ride to the hospital, the limb

13
00:00:47,724 --> 00:00:49,441
is almost always fine.

14
00:00:49,491 --> 00:00:52,928
And waiting? That's
what gets people killed.

15
00:00:52,978 --> 00:00:58,008
So today, a life threatening bleed
from an arm or a leg gets a tourniquet

16
00:00:58,058 --> 00:00:58,947
right away.

17
00:01:01,997 --> 00:01:06,160
Before you treat it, look at it,
because the blood tells you which vessel

18
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:10,547
is open. Arterial blood is bright
red, because it's full of oxygen.

19
00:01:10,597 --> 00:01:14,567
And it spurts with every heartbeat,
because the heart is pushing it right

20
00:01:14,617 --> 00:01:19,200
out. It's under high pressure, so this
is the one that can kill fast, and

21
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:20,757
it's the toughest to stop.

22
00:01:21,257 --> 00:01:25,840
Venous blood is darker red, and it flows
steadily instead of spurting, because

23
00:01:25,890 --> 00:01:27,640
there's not much pressure behind it.

24
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:30,577
It can still be heavy
if a big vein is open.

25
00:01:30,627 --> 00:01:35,247
And capillary bleeding is a slow,
dark red ooze from the surface, like a

26
00:01:35,297 --> 00:01:37,917
scraped knee. It usually stops on its own.

27
00:01:38,417 --> 00:01:41,014
Now, how much blood are we talking about?

28
00:01:41,064 --> 00:01:43,547
An adult has about five liters.

29
00:01:43,597 --> 00:01:48,417
If an adult suddenly loses about one
liter, roughly a fifth of it, that's

30
00:01:48,467 --> 00:01:54,307
serious. A child has a lot less, so around
five hundred milliliters is serious,

31
00:01:54,357 --> 00:01:57,609
and for an infant it's just
a hundred to two hundred.

32
00:01:57,659 --> 00:02:01,451
So the same puddle on the floor is
a very different problem with a baby

33
00:02:01,501 --> 00:02:02,837
than with a grown man.

34
00:02:03,337 --> 00:02:05,767
So what makes a bleed life threatening?

35
00:02:05,817 --> 00:02:09,944
It's spurting, or it's pooling on
the floor fast, or it soaks through a

36
00:02:09,994 --> 00:02:13,420
dressing in seconds, or
part of the limb is gone.

37
00:02:13,470 --> 00:02:15,990
Any of those, and you're
thinking tourniquet.

38
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:21,693
Quick check. Dark red blood, flowing
steadily from a deep cut on the shin.

39
00:02:21,743 --> 00:02:24,530
No spurting. Which kind of vessel is open?

40
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:33,120
A vein. Dark and steady, that's a vein.

41
00:02:33,170 --> 00:02:36,330
If it were bright and spurting,
you'd be thinking artery.

42
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:42,260
So why does pushing on
a wound work at all?

43
00:02:42,310 --> 00:02:45,330
Picture the torn vessel under the skin.

44
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:49,890
When you press, you squeeze the tissue
around it, and the vessel gets pinched

45
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:54,780
flatter. Less blood gets through the
hole, and it comes out with less force.

46
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:57,510
And that's what buys you time.

47
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:00,443
Your body already knows
how to plug a hole.

48
00:03:00,493 --> 00:03:05,206
Platelets, tiny cell pieces in your
blood that help it clot, stick to the

49
00:03:05,256 --> 00:03:08,270
torn edge, and a clot builds across it.

50
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:12,354
But a clot can't hold while
blood is blasting through.

51
00:03:12,404 --> 00:03:16,250
Pressure slows the flow
enough for the clot to form.

52
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:18,560
And most of the time, that's enough.

53
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:21,260
You can do it three ways.

54
00:03:21,310 --> 00:03:25,630
You can just use your gloved hand, or
you can use your gloved hand to hold

55
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:30,810
a dressing down, or you can put on a
pressure dressing, which is a bandage

56
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:35,000
wrapped firm enough that it keeps
pressing even after you let go.

57
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:40,620
So step one is to press hard.

58
00:03:40,670 --> 00:03:45,886
Use your palm for a big wound and
your fingers for a small one, and push

59
00:03:45,936 --> 00:03:50,672
toward a bone underneath if there is
one, so the vessel gets squeezed against

60
00:03:50,722 --> 00:03:55,392
something firm. If it's spurting,
don't go looking for gauze.

61
00:03:55,442 --> 00:04:01,102
Your gloved hand goes on the wound
now, and on an arm or a leg, you keep

62
00:04:01,152 --> 00:04:03,210
pressing while the tourniquet comes out.

63
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:06,952
Then step two is you hold it.

64
00:04:07,002 --> 00:04:12,112
For massive bleeding, you keep that
pressure on all the way to the hospital.

65
00:04:12,162 --> 00:04:16,999
For a smaller bleed, you hold it steady
without peeking until the bleeding

66
00:04:17,049 --> 00:04:17,849
stops.

67
00:04:18,202 --> 00:04:21,852
Step three is the one people get wrong.

68
00:04:21,902 --> 00:04:25,512
Don't start by building
a big stack of gauze.

69
00:04:25,562 --> 00:04:30,912
A tall stack just soaks up blood,
and it puts cushion between your hand

70
00:04:30,962 --> 00:04:34,322
and the wound, so you're
actually pressing less.

71
00:04:34,822 --> 00:04:39,849
Step four is, once the bleeding's under
control, you turn it into a pressure

72
00:04:39,899 --> 00:04:45,089
dressing. Wrap the bandage firmly,
so the pressure stays on without your

73
00:04:45,139 --> 00:04:45,939
hands.

74
00:04:46,226 --> 00:04:51,516
And step five. Don't peel
off a dressing once it's on.

75
00:04:51,566 --> 00:04:56,440
The clot is forming in that gauze, and
ripping it off tears the clot right

76
00:04:56,490 --> 00:05:02,280
back open. If blood soaks through,
leave it there and press harder.

77
00:05:02,330 --> 00:05:04,706
That just means you need to do more.

78
00:05:05,206 --> 00:05:11,009
So what's more? On an arm or a leg,
if pressure isn't stopping it, that's

79
00:05:11,059 --> 00:05:16,745
a tourniquet. And in the spots where
a tourniquet can't go, you pack the

80
00:05:16,795 --> 00:05:22,729
wound, with plain gauze or hemostatic
gauze, gauze that helps the blood

81
00:05:22,779 --> 00:05:27,219
clot. But a life threatening limb bleed
doesn't wait for pressure to fail.

82
00:05:30,269 --> 00:05:33,947
There's one bleed where instinct gets
it wrong, and you'll see it all the

83
00:05:33,997 --> 00:05:38,707
time. The nosebleed. Most
people tip the head back.

84
00:05:38,757 --> 00:05:43,367
Don't. The blood just runs down the
throat, it gets swallowed, it irritates

85
00:05:43,417 --> 00:05:46,557
the stomach, and now your
bleeding patient is vomiting too.

86
00:05:47,057 --> 00:05:50,916
Do the opposite. Sit them
upright and lean them forward.

87
00:05:50,966 --> 00:05:56,068
Then pinch the soft, fleshy part
of the nose, below the bony bridge.

88
00:05:56,118 --> 00:06:01,033
Most nosebleeds start near the front,
in that soft part, and bone can't

89
00:06:01,083 --> 00:06:04,942
be squeezed. So you just
lean them forward and pinch.

90
00:06:07,992 --> 00:06:12,724
Back to the garage. His blood is bright
red and spurting, and it's pushing

91
00:06:12,774 --> 00:06:15,222
straight through the gauze under her hand.

92
00:06:15,272 --> 00:06:19,729
That's a life threatening bleed from
a limb, so he gets a tourniquet.

93
00:06:19,779 --> 00:06:20,992
Right now.

94
00:06:21,492 --> 00:06:24,449
Use a commercial windlass tourniquet.

95
00:06:24,499 --> 00:06:29,254
There's a wide strap that goes around
the limb, and a rod, called the windlass,

96
00:06:29,304 --> 00:06:31,110
that you twist to tighten it.

97
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:34,832
Then there's a clip that locks
the rod so it can't unwind.

98
00:06:35,332 --> 00:06:38,422
Why not a belt, or a shirt and a stick?

99
00:06:38,472 --> 00:06:42,022
Those improvised ones
fail a lot more often.

100
00:06:42,072 --> 00:06:46,902
They're usually too narrow to press hard
enough on the artery, so they squeeze

101
00:06:46,952 --> 00:06:49,922
the veins shut, but not the artery.

102
00:06:49,972 --> 00:06:52,795
And that can make the bleeding worse.

103
00:06:52,845 --> 00:06:55,195
We'll get to why in a minute.

104
00:06:55,245 --> 00:06:59,512
So only make one if you
really have nothing else.

105
00:07:02,562 --> 00:07:03,872
So, where does it go?

106
00:07:03,922 --> 00:07:07,939
You put it two to three inches above
the wound, between the wound and the

107
00:07:07,989 --> 00:07:11,552
heart, on healthy skin,
and never over a joint.

108
00:07:11,602 --> 00:07:16,152
At the elbow or the knee, bone and
tendon shield the artery, so the strap

109
00:07:16,202 --> 00:07:17,652
can't squeeze it shut.

110
00:07:17,702 --> 00:07:22,212
His cut is near the wrist, so it goes
on his forearm, a few inches toward

111
00:07:22,262 --> 00:07:23,062
the elbow.

112
00:07:23,342 --> 00:07:27,485
You do it differently if it's an
amputation, if there's more than one wound

113
00:07:27,535 --> 00:07:31,478
on the same limb, or if the limb's so
mangled you can't tell where the blood's

114
00:07:31,528 --> 00:07:36,642
coming from. Then you go high and tight,
as high up on the limb as you can.

115
00:07:37,142 --> 00:07:39,332
Now, how tight does it need to be?

116
00:07:39,382 --> 00:07:44,352
You twist until the bleeding stops,
and the distal pulse is gone.

117
00:07:44,402 --> 00:07:48,072
That's the pulse below the
tourniquet, at the wrist or the foot.

118
00:07:48,122 --> 00:07:52,922
You need both, and to see why, look
at what's going on inside the arm.

119
00:07:53,422 --> 00:07:57,692
Blood comes into the arm through an
artery, pushed hard by the heart.

120
00:07:57,742 --> 00:08:00,999
It goes back out through
veins, at low pressure.

121
00:08:01,049 --> 00:08:04,552
So a loose strap squeezes
the veins shut first.

122
00:08:04,602 --> 00:08:07,399
But the artery is still pumping blood in.

123
00:08:07,449 --> 00:08:12,469
Blood gets in and can't get out, so
the limb fills up, and the wound bleeds

124
00:08:12,519 --> 00:08:16,652
harder. Twist further,
and the artery closes too.

125
00:08:16,702 --> 00:08:21,862
Now nothing gets in. The bleeding
stops, and the pulse below is gone.

126
00:08:22,362 --> 00:08:23,597
And it's going to hurt.

127
00:08:23,647 --> 00:08:27,947
A lot, usually. Tell him
that before you start.

128
00:08:27,997 --> 00:08:30,167
But don't loosen it just because it hurts.

129
00:08:30,667 --> 00:08:35,617
Quick check. A spurting cut on the
lower leg, just below the knee.

130
00:08:35,667 --> 00:08:39,617
If you measure two to three inches
up, you end up right on the knee.

131
00:08:39,667 --> 00:08:41,440
So where does the tourniquet go?

132
00:08:47,940 --> 00:08:50,223
On the thigh, above the knee.

133
00:08:50,273 --> 00:08:52,583
Never on the joint itself.

134
00:08:52,633 --> 00:08:56,628
If your two to three inches lands
on a joint, just go above the joint.

135
00:08:59,678 --> 00:09:04,732
Once it's on, write down the time on the
tourniquet itself and in your report.

136
00:09:04,782 --> 00:09:07,488
Then say it out loud at handoff.

137
00:09:07,538 --> 00:09:12,401
The longer it's on, the bigger the
risk to the limb, so the hospital needs

138
00:09:12,451 --> 00:09:13,251
to know.

139
00:09:13,498 --> 00:09:15,988
Leave it where people can see it.

140
00:09:16,038 --> 00:09:18,615
Never cover it with a
blanket or a dressing.

141
00:09:18,665 --> 00:09:21,335
The next person shouldn't
have to hunt for it.

142
00:09:21,385 --> 00:09:24,702
And don't loosen it or
take it off in the field.

143
00:09:24,752 --> 00:09:27,245
Loosen it, and the bleeding starts again.

144
00:09:27,745 --> 00:09:33,088
If it's as tight as it'll go and he's
still bleeding, don't keep cranking.

145
00:09:33,138 --> 00:09:37,678
Put a second tourniquet on, right
above the first, closer to the heart.

146
00:09:40,728 --> 00:09:45,038
In some places, like the groin or the
armpit, there's just nothing to wrap

147
00:09:45,088 --> 00:09:49,898
a tourniquet around. Those are called
junctional areas, where a limb meets

148
00:09:49,948 --> 00:09:50,748
the body.

149
00:09:51,028 --> 00:09:56,751
So you pack it. Push gauze into the
wound, plain or hemostatic, all the

150
00:09:56,801 --> 00:10:00,811
way down to where the blood is coming
from, and keep feeding it in until

151
00:10:00,861 --> 00:10:02,801
the space is packed tight.

152
00:10:03,301 --> 00:10:05,747
Then press hard on top.

153
00:10:05,797 --> 00:10:09,939
With hemostatic gauze,
at least three minutes.

154
00:10:09,989 --> 00:10:14,232
It speeds up clotting, but only where
it's actually touching the bleeding,

155
00:10:14,282 --> 00:10:17,571
so it needs to be in there,
and it needs that time.

156
00:10:17,621 --> 00:10:22,163
With plain gauze, keep pressing until
the bleeding's controlled, or you

157
00:10:22,213 --> 00:10:28,059
hand off. If you just lay gauze on
top, that's a dressing, not packing,

158
00:10:28,109 --> 00:10:30,189
and it won't stop a bleed like this.

159
00:10:30,689 --> 00:10:35,812
There are two places you never
pack: the chest and the belly.

160
00:10:35,862 --> 00:10:39,172
There's nothing solid to
pack against in there.

161
00:10:39,222 --> 00:10:43,580
The gauze can end up somewhere you
can't control, and you can't press on

162
00:10:43,630 --> 00:10:45,512
what's bleeding inside.

163
00:10:45,562 --> 00:10:48,252
The neck is different again.

164
00:10:48,302 --> 00:10:53,265
A torn vein in the neck can suck in
air, so you cover it with an occlusive

165
00:10:53,315 --> 00:10:58,479
dressing, one that seals air
out, and press on that one side.

166
00:10:58,529 --> 00:11:01,382
Never wrap anything around the neck.

167
00:11:04,432 --> 00:11:06,675
So, putting all of that together.

168
00:11:06,725 --> 00:11:12,078
If it's small or slow,
you press, hold, and wrap.

169
00:11:12,128 --> 00:11:16,622
If it soaks through, leave
the dressing and do more.

170
00:11:16,672 --> 00:11:22,366
If an arm or a leg is bleeding badly
enough to kill, the tourniquet goes

171
00:11:22,416 --> 00:11:28,835
on first. It goes two to three inches
above, never on a joint, and you tighten

172
00:11:28,885 --> 00:11:32,995
it until the bleeding stops
and the pulse below is gone.

173
00:11:33,045 --> 00:11:37,322
In the groin or armpit,
you pack it and press.

174
00:11:37,372 --> 00:11:41,672
And for a nosebleed, lean
them forward and pinch.

175
00:11:44,722 --> 00:11:49,412
Problem one. You've held pressure on a
forearm cut, and blood starts soaking

176
00:11:49,462 --> 00:11:53,452
through your gauze. Your partner reaches
to peel it off and put on a fresh

177
00:11:53,502 --> 00:11:55,002
one. What do you do?

178
00:12:01,502 --> 00:12:04,743
Stop them. Leave it on.

179
00:12:04,793 --> 00:12:07,703
Peeling it off tears
the clot that's forming.

180
00:12:07,753 --> 00:12:12,646
Press harder, and if it still won't
stop, that forearm gets a tourniquet.

181
00:12:13,146 --> 00:12:17,796
Problem two. You tightened
a tourniquet on a thigh.

182
00:12:17,846 --> 00:12:22,436
The bleeding slowed but didn't stop,
and the foot still has a pulse.

183
00:12:22,486 --> 00:12:23,726
What's wrong?

184
00:12:30,226 --> 00:12:32,389
It isn't tight enough.

185
00:12:32,439 --> 00:12:37,765
The veins are shut, but the artery
isn't, so blood keeps coming in.

186
00:12:37,815 --> 00:12:42,409
Keep twisting until the bleeding
stops and the pulse is gone.

187
00:12:42,459 --> 00:12:46,479
And if it won't go any tighter,
add a second one above it.

188
00:12:46,979 --> 00:12:52,849
Problem three. A stab wound in
the groin, and it's pouring.

189
00:12:52,899 --> 00:12:54,819
Tourniquet, or pack it?

190
00:13:00,319 --> 00:13:05,597
Pack it. The groin is junctional,
so there's no limb to wrap.

191
00:13:05,647 --> 00:13:10,381
Get the gauze in, all the way down to
where it's bleeding, pack it tight,

192
00:13:10,431 --> 00:13:14,817
and then press hard. A lot of people
grab the tourniquet here, because it's

193
00:13:14,867 --> 00:13:17,767
the big tool. But it has nowhere to go.

194
00:13:20,817 --> 00:13:21,940
So that's external bleeding.

195
00:13:21,990 --> 00:13:26,127
The notes and practice for this
topic are free on levlprep.com.

196
00:13:26,177 --> 00:13:31,309
In the next video we'll get into the
bleeding you can't see, which is internal

197
00:13:31,359 --> 00:13:35,917
bleeding, and how to catch shock
before the blood pressure drops.

198
00:13:35,967 --> 00:13:36,767
See you there.
